; GROUPS
;
; A group is a set G with an operation ∘ that is associative, has a neutral element e, and in
; which every element a has an inverse inv(a). This scene asks for as little as possible: e is
; only neutral from the LEFT (e ∘ a = a), and inv(a) is only an inverse from the LEFT
; (inv(a) ∘ a = e). Exercise 1 shows that this is enough: both work from the right too.
;
;     G               the set of the group's elements
;     a ∘ b           the operation (binds stronger than = and ∈: `a ∘ b = c` is `(a ∘ b) = c`)
;     e               the neutral element
;     inv(a)          the inverse of a
;
; The rules (with set.kurt and equality.kurt, which it loads):
;
;     "closed"         a ∈ G ∧ b ∈ G  ⇒  a ∘ b ∈ G
;     "e-in-G"         e ∈ G
;     "inv-in-G"       a ∈ G  ⇒  inv(a) ∈ G
;     "assoc"          a ∈ G ∧ b ∈ G ∧ c ∈ G  ⇒  (a ∘ b) ∘ c = a ∘ (b ∘ c)
;     "left-neutral"   a ∈ G  ⇒  e ∘ a = a
;     "left-inverse"   a ∈ G  ⇒  inv(a) ∘ a = e
;
; Kurt applies a rule only when its conditions are facts: before you use, say, "assoc" for
; a, inv(a) and b, the lines `a ∈ G`, `inv(a) ∈ G` and `b ∈ G` must be there -- write them as
; lines of your proof ("inv-in-G" gives `inv(a) ∈ G`, "closed" gives `a ∘ b ∈ G`). If Kurt can't
; derive an equation and mentions `extensionality`, a fact `... ∈ G` is usually missing.
;
; Calculations: write an equation as a chain, one step per line, each step one rule
;
;     a = e ∘ a                  ; left-neutral
;       = (inv(b) ∘ b) ∘ a       ; left-inverse
;       = inv(b) ∘ (b ∘ a)       ; assoc
;
; and Kurt concludes the equation from the first to the last term (`a = inv(b) ∘ (b ∘ a)`).
; Each step rewrites the previous term with one equation -- also the first, which rewrites its
; left side: `a ∘ e = a ∘ (inv(a) ∘ a)` by "left-inverse", inside the term.
;
; This file is the scene of the exercises: they load it with `load theory/groups-scene`. Don't
; change it.
load set
infix ∘ 70 70
arity inv 1
const G, e, ∘, inv

use $a ∈ G ∧ $b ∈ G  ⇒  $a ∘ $b ∈ G                                  "closed"
use e ∈ G                                                            "e-in-G"
use $a ∈ G  ⇒  inv($a) ∈ G                                           "inv-in-G"
use $a ∈ G ∧ $b ∈ G ∧ $c ∈ G  ⇒  ($a ∘ $b) ∘ $c = $a ∘ ($b ∘ $c)     "assoc"
use $a ∈ G  ⇒  e ∘ $a = $a                                           "left-neutral"
use $a ∈ G  ⇒  inv($a) ∘ $a = e                                      "left-inverse"
